## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical problem solving for shared Linux runtimes. It focuses on beginner-friendly CPU isolation using cgroup v2 primitives rather than app changes.

## Key Concepts
- cgroup v2 CPU controller (cpu.max, cpu.weight)
- systemd slices or cpuset for per-tenant isolation
- basic benchmarking of latency under bursts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Reads current cpu.max for a given cgroup path
const fs = require('fs');
try {
  const quota = fs.readFileSync('/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.slice/cpu.max','utf8').trim();
  console.log('cpu.max:', quota);
} catch (e) { console.error(e); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this plan to handle bursty tenants with varying baselines?
- What risks exist with aggressive quotas and how would you detect them?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests Linux networking, virtualization concepts, and QoS under multi-tenant load in a real-world VPN gateway.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant namespaces/VRF routing
- XDP/BPF for ACLs and QoS
- tc/qdisc HTB or fq_codel per tenant
- IRQ affinity and CPU pinning
- NUMA awareness and tenant cgroups
- Observability with iperf/netperf

## Code Example

```javascript
// High-level pseudo steps (not executed as-is)
const tenants = ["t1","t2"];
for (const t of tenants) {
  const ns = createNamespace(`vn_${t}`);
  setupVRF(ns, t);
  bindVpnWorkerToNamespace(ns);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would tail latency be measured and bounded at 99th percentile?
- How to safely onboard/offboard tenants without disruption?
